{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"quiz-night-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Quiz night track","description":"Takes a quiz night from brief to the last scores in gated steps - format and audience, writing the rounds, an editor's check, printed and on-screen materials, and the host's run of the night.","category":"trivia","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","build","operate"],"role":["individual","teacher","gamer"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["preferences","text"],"output":["quiz","plan","checklist","script"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","depth":"specific","tags":["pub-quiz","quiz-night","fundraiser-quiz","answer-sheets","run-sheet","verify-list"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["host-trivia-night","review-quiz-questions","design-quiz-scoring-rules","plan-picture-round"]},"args":[{"name":"event","description":"What the night is, where and for whom, for example \"school PTA fundraiser quiz in the hall, about 15 tables of 6 adults\".","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"date","description":"When it happens.","type":"string","required":true},{"name":"theme","description":"General knowledge, or a theme and any rounds you already want. Optional.","type":"string"}],"steps":[{"id":"brief","file":"steps/01-brief.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"write-rounds","file":"steps/02-write-rounds.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve"},{"id":"editor-check","file":"steps/03-editor-check.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve"},{"id":"materials","file":"steps/04-materials.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve"},{"id":"run-the-night","file":"steps/05-run-the-night.md","stage":"operate","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Prepares a quiz night the way an experienced quizmaster does, pausing after each step for approval. The format comes first because it decides how much to write; the editor's check happens before anything is printed.\n\n<event>\n{{event}}\n</event>\nDate: {{date}}\n{{#theme}}Theme: {{theme}}{{/theme}}\n\nThroughout: every answer is one you are confident of; anything time-sensitive or uncertain carries [verify] and the date your knowledge reflects. Never invent prices, venue rules or licences; if the night charges entry, runs a raffle or plays recorded music in public, list it as something to check with the venue or local authority. Keep printed material short and readable.","steps":[{"id":"brief","file":"steps/01-brief.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Agree the brief\n\n1. Read the event. If the audience (ages, country, casual or keen), the number of teams or the time available is unclear, ask up to four questions in one message and wait.\n2. Propose the format: number of rounds and questions, which rounds are special (picture handout, music, connections, a final round worth more), whether there is a joker, and the scoring in three lines.\n3. Draft the timing: start, rounds of about 10 to 12 minutes, marking time, a break, scores and prizes, finishing time.\n4. List what the host needs: a co-host or marker, a microphone, a screen or printed sheets, pens, a scoreboard.\n\nStop and ask the person to approve the format before writing questions."},{"id":"write-rounds","file":"steps/02-write-rounds.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Write the rounds\n\n1. Write every round from the approved format. Within each round order questions easy to hard (about 30, 50 and 20 percent); across the night start accessible and build.\n2. Each question has one answer; give \"also accept\" alternatives and what not to accept.\n3. Vary subjects so no kind of player dominates; keep local or generational questions to a few, matched to the audience.\n4. For a picture or music round, describe each item and how to prepare it fairly; do not reproduce lyrics or point to unofficial downloads.\n5. Write three numeric tie-breakers with stable answers.\n\nDeliver complete rounds; if they do not fit in one reply, say which remain and continue when asked. Stop for approval."},{"id":"editor-check","file":"steps/03-editor-check.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Editor's check\n\nReview the approved rounds as a strict quiz editor would.\n\n1. Flag questions with a second valid answer, ambiguous wording, a likely myth, a giveaway, or a fact that may have changed.\n2. Check balance per round: at least three gettable questions, no run of one subject.\n3. Rewrite each flagged question, and gather every [verify] item into one list with the kind of source to check it against.\n\nStop and ask the person to confirm the [verify] items are checked before materials are made."},{"id":"materials","file":"steps/04-materials.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Make the materials\n\n1. Team answer sheets: one per round, team name box, numbered lines, joker box if used.\n2. Picture handout layout if there is one.\n3. Host script: welcome, rules read in under a minute, each question as read aloud, pronunciation notes, one fun fact for selected answers.\n4. Marker's answer key, compact, by round, with \"also accept\".\n5. Scoresheet: teams by rounds, running total, rank.\n6. On-screen text if a screen is used: one question per slide, large font.\n\nStop for approval."},{"id":"run-the-night","file":"steps/05-run-the-night.md","stage":"operate","gate":"none","text":"# Step 5: Run the night\n\n1. A run sheet from setup to pack-down: arrival, table setup, sound check, each round, marking windows, break, final scores, prizes.\n2. A dispute rule: the host's answer stands unless a team shows the question allowed another answer; rulings apply to every team equally.\n3. What to do if behind time: drop a round or shorten one, never rush the final.\n4. A short after-note: thank-you message wording, and a list of questions that caused disputes to fix next time."}]}
